\b\fs28 Watch Flag and script:\b0\fs22\par
  "scripts": \{\par
    "start": "node src/index.js",\par
    "dev": "node --watch src/index.js"\par
  \},\par
or\par
\b\fs24 install as a dev dependency:\b0  \fs22 npm i nodemon --save-dev\par
add this in script:\par
 "scripts": \{\par
    "dev": "nodemon src/index.js",\par
  \},\par
\par
\b\fs28 File Structure:\b0\fs22\par
config is going to help us have the database connection\par
\par
\b\fs28 Verification middleware:\b0\fs22\par
This code is like a security check for your routes.\par
When someone tries to use a protected route, this code looks for a token in the request headers (Authorization: Bearer <token>).\par
If no token \f1\u8594?\f0  \f2\u10060?\f0  access denied.\par
If a token is there \f1\u8594?\f0  it checks if the token is real and not fake/expired using your secret key.\par
If the token is valid \f1\u8594?\f0  \f2\u9989?\f0  user is allowed in, and their info is stored in req.user.\par
If the token is invalid \f1\u8594?\f0  \f2\u10060?\f0  access denied.\par
\f3\u-10179?\u-9143?\f0  Purpose: Make sure only logged-in users with a valid token can access certain routes.\lang9\par
\par
req.user is just a property we add to the request object (req).\par
When the token is verified, it contains the user\rquote s info (like id, role, etc.).\par
We put that info into req.user.\par
That way, in your protected routes, you can easily know which user is making the request.\par
\b\par
\fs32 TOKEN FOR PROTECTED:\fs22\par
\b0 What\rquote s happening?\par
Right now, your verifyToken middleware only checks if \b the token is valid.\b0\par
It does not check the role (admin vs user).\par
So:\par
If you log in as admin \f1\u8594?\f0  you get an admin token.\par
When you hit \b /admin\b0 , middleware verifies token \f2\u9989?\f0  and you see "Welcome admin".\par
When you hit \b /user\b0  with the same admin token, middleware still verifies token \f2\u9989?\f0  and allows the request, so you see "Welcome user".\par
That\rquote s why you get the decoded admin info in req.user, but the route handler just sends the "Welcome user" message anyway.\par
Why no error/catch?\par
Because your code only throws an error if:\par

\pard\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Token is missing, or\par
Token is invalid.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 It does not throw an error for wrong role, because you haven\rquote t told it to.\par
THEREFORE, IF YOU HAVE A VALID TOKEN IRRESPECTIVE OF THE ROLE, YOU WILL BE ABLE TO ACCESS THE ROUTE.  \par
